{
  // THE RUNTIME-NEUTRAL PROJECT. Spec §3.1: the console is a browser bundle, so
  // one `node:` import or one `document` in here and the console can never
  // adopt this directory, the flow in §2 quietly dies, and whoever tries a year
  // from now is the one who finds out.
  //
  // Two fields carry that, and they are the compile-time half of the guard task
  // 2 writes the scan half of:
  //
  //   - `lib` is ES2022 and nothing else. No DOM, no DOM.Iterable, so
  //     `document`, `window`, `HTMLElement` and `DocumentFragment` are not
  //     names this project has.
  //   - `types` is empty, so no @types package is pulled in automatically.
  //     `process` is not a global here. If a later task installs @types/node
  //     for `view/`, THIS LINE is what stops it arriving here too — it must
  //     stay empty.
  //
  //     <b>TASK 7 INSTALLED @types/node FOR `view/`, AND ONE HALF OF THAT LINE
  //     IS NO LONGER TRUE.</b> `types: []` stops the package arriving as
  //     ambient globals — `process` is still TS2591 here, measured — but it
  //     does NOT stop an explicit `import … from 'node:fs'` resolving through
  //     the package on disk. Measured on the day of that edit: a used
  //     `readFileSync` import planted in `markdown.ts` and `tsc -b --force`
  //     EXITS 0, where the line below records TS2307. So for that one rule the
  //     compiler is no longer a guard and `src/neutrality.test.ts` is the
  //     whole of it — which is the eventuality that file was written for and
  //     says so in its own header.
  //
  // `references` is absent on purpose and not merely unneeded. This project
  // does not reference `../binding` or `../view`, so an import of either is
  // refused. The dependencies run one way (spec §3) and the compiler is told
  // so.
  //
  // MEASURED, each rule broken on its own in this file and `tsc -b --force`
  // run, because a guard that has never refused anything proves nothing:
  //
  //   import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'   TS2307 (NO LONGER — see below)
  //   process.cwd()                            TS2591 (NO LONGER — see below)
  //   document.title                           TS2584 cannot find name
  //   import from '../view/wiring.ts'          TS6059 + TS6307
  //   import from '../binding/wiring.ts'       TS6059 + TS6307
  //
  // <b>RE-MEASURED 2026-09-12, AND FIVE OF SIX ARE GONE.</b> Planting each
  // probe on its own and running `tsc -b --force`:
  //
  //   node: import   expected TS2307   COMPILES CLEAN
  //   process        expected TS2591   COMPILES CLEAN
  //   document       expected TS2584   TS2584          (the only survivor)
  //   WebSocket      expected TS2304   COMPILES CLEAN
  //   fetch          expected TS2304   COMPILES CLEAN
  //   setTimeout     expected TS2304   COMPILES CLEAN
  //
  // <b>AND REPAIRED THE SAME DAY. All six refuse again</b>, measured the same
  // way after the tests moved into `tsconfig.test.json`:
  //
  //   node: import   TS2307     process   TS2591     document     TS2584
  //   WebSocket      TS2304     fetch     TS2304     setTimeout   TS2304
  //
  // Including the `node:` import, which the paragraph above had written off as
  // permanently lost to @types/node. It was not lost to the package; it was
  // lost to the test files that pull the package in, and excluding them gets
  // it back.
  //
  // <b>And the cause is `include`, not `types`.</b> `**/*.ts` takes in this
  // directory's test files; each imports `vitest`; and a .d.ts reached through
  // an explicit import contributes its GLOBAL declarations to the entire
  // program, `skipLibCheck` notwithstanding. So every file here can see Node's
  // globals because its neighbours' tests asked for them. Measured by excluding
  // `**/*.test.ts` and re-running the `process` probe: TS2591 comes straight
  // back.
  //
  // `document` survives because `lib` is a different mechanism — it is the set
  // of ambient libraries for this project and no import can widen it.
  //
  // <b>Both guards are live now, and neither is redundant.</b> The compiler
  // reads the type graph and the scan reads the source text: `declare const
  // process: never` gets past the first, and a `.d.ts` dragging in an ambient
  // global gets past it too under `skipLibCheck`. The four rules added to
  // `src/neutrality.test.ts` on the day the compiler was found blind — fetch,
  // WebSocket, setTimeout, setInterval — stay, because a guard that was earning
  // its keep alone is not one to remove when its partner recovers.
  //
  // <b>The first thing the repaired compiler caught</b> was real: `binding/`'s
  // `eventsUrl` was building a query with `URLSearchParams`, a platform type
  // that `types: []` is supposed to make unnameable there, and it compiled only
  // because of this leak. It encodes by hand now.
  //
  // Every one of the five named `src/logic/wiring.ts` and a line number, at the
  // time each was taken; task 3 and task 7 have since deleted every `wiring.ts`
  // in the module, so the file names are history rather than a path to go and
  // look at. The four still-live rules re-measure against any file here. The
  // two hint texts are worth knowing about: TS2591 suggests installing
  // @types/node and adding it to `types`, and TS2584 suggests adding `dom` to
  // `lib`. <b>Both pieces of advice are wrong in this directory</b> — following
  // either is the exact defect §3.1 is about — which is why they are written
  // down here beside the field the advice would have somebody edit.
  //
  // Why this is not the whole guard, which is worth saying so task 2 is not
  // mistaken for belt-and-braces: the compiler only objects to what it must
  // resolve. `declare const process: never` or a string that happens to spell a
  // global gets past it, and `skipLibCheck` means a .d.ts dragging in an
  // ambient Node global would too. The scan reads the source text; this reads
  // the type graph. Neither subsumes the other.
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "types": [],
    "rootDir": ".",
    "outDir": "../../build/types/logic",
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "../../build/types/logic/.tsbuildinfo"
  },

  // TESTS ARE NOT IN THIS PROJECT, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES `types: []` TRUE.
  //
  // Measured on 2026-09-12: with `**/*.ts` taking in this directory's own test
  // files, five of the six probes below compiled clean. The cause was not
  // `types` -- it was `include`. Each test imports vitest, and a .d.ts reached
  // through an explicit import contributes its GLOBAL declarations to the
  // entire program, `skipLibCheck` notwithstanding. So every file here could
  // see `process`, `fetch`, `WebSocket` and the timers because its neighbours'
  // tests asked for them.
  //
  // The tests are still typechecked -- `tsconfig.test.json` beside this file
  // compiles them together with these sources and node's types -- so nothing is
  // unchecked. What changed is that THIS program, the one whose neutrality is
  // the point, no longer has a reason to know what a `process` is.
  "exclude": ["**/*.test.ts"],
  "include": ["**/*.ts"]
}
